#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04dde756-d3cb-5b12-b2e8-19a55ecbec4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589238} {:answer/id #uuid "5bddb1b9-4044-5bf9-a050-b260ca4ca2fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589453} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8dcae3-c38a-593f-a7d8-8b12b3dd6212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589483} {:answer/id #uuid "9956c7fe-7952-5ec3-b4ca-8979086d4fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589582} {:answer/id #uuid "ce613482-5956-5313-bf7a-c9e80cfa04f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589691}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186589483} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8183c5b-6091-5078-832b-834e33f394d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d4373777-4c0c-5185-ac6a-65ddd60853a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-96294" :question/problem "Given that the continuous random variable $U$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{3}{2}],$ compute $P(U\\ge 0).$" :question/worked-solution "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $U$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le u\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $U$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{3}{2}],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & -\\frac{5}{2}\\le u\\le \\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(U\\ge 0) & =P(0\\le U\\le \\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}f(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2}-0)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4154106d-ff01-5ce5-821a-6ab59f244526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589398} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9d3e7d-9409-5357-9d2e-87769ffa212d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589493} {:answer/id #uuid "8891af16-9a0c-52dc-9fd3-7002acb38f5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589544} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae26c6c-27c5-5b87-91b4-dbf989a728b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589548} {:answer/id #uuid "9aae324f-9e8b-50bf-8b7a-2eb50789b9f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186589548} :answer-field/id #uuid "fca82219-9297-52ca-adc0-2955bb84a9f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f037d7df-ebc1-5173-841d-2662ff41eae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-143863" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{XY}{3}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-3x; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-3x\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/b5/b5a9355f0c2a39a1da71f3132c1eb3d7c72464af62b23f8a26d76df306bf9773.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{XY}{3}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}\\frac{xy}{3}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{3}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3-3x}\\frac{xy}{3}\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-3x}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=3-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(3-3x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{2}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =3[\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "848c7a13-dbfb-5041-a74c-75500c8b3bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589534} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff5d011-113e-5e19-8a74-6abdb7e3f48f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589558} {:answer/id #uuid "986e4a1c-b5b5-51ea-9dd7-e5d12951d9f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589576} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ffbda4-1ecb-56a6-bf5c-471ccd0df0cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589745} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f95022-2473-56c7-beb4-b339c4ca69a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589782}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186589745} :answer-field/id #uuid "907787c7-6367-5897-a516-f38d7ab9fe24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fb541831-9b3a-5fd7-8e37-b74e4a193596" :question/math-academy-id "q-146193" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,4)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{6}, & \\,y=2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}, & \\,y=4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=4]$ given that the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,4)$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of  $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\sum }_{y}y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=4] & ={\\sum }_{y}y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,4) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19320835-9a45-55e6-a00a-53fdbefbc7e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589289} {:answer/id #uuid "2b2dd721-06fa-5888-b332-ea19bef0641f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589341} {:answer/id #uuid "b9cdee57-6749-5a2a-ac5b-b3f81e283df4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589643} {:answer/id #uuid "c7016267-fccb-5da2-b87d-6cd07e440ae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589673} {:answer/id #uuid "e2602d27-c46d-5e7c-a7c5-d4fdce7161da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589730}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186589730} :answer-field/id #uuid "a67221f6-fb73-5b09-9336-ce2d59da7bd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fff8694e-6759-5587-9bc4-103657c55516" :question/math-academy-id "q-130539" :question/problem "Given that the continuous random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-4,0],$ compute $P(X<-\\sqrt{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-4,0],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & -4\\le x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-\\sqrt{2}) & =P(-4\\le X<-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{-\\sqrt{2}}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{-\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}-(-4)}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02cda1c9-b0ce-53f9-90ec-4c01349fc2d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0431" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590191} {:answer/id #uuid "1ae8c91d-3349-5934-b917-6de5f140d8f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0331" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590222} {:answer/id #uuid "46a67834-8ee1-5cfb-be9b-6156ecd5eec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3431" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590315} {:answer/id #uuid "7d58385b-e4f2-5f8e-b4d2-c877af5ed2e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0411" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590394} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a805ce-4a88-5e52-972a-04239ff27c08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1431" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590526}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590191} :answer-field/id #uuid "4532f29e-b252-5467-b41e-cc5fd38caed1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0ce2375f-f324-5d66-88d2-48bdd2d9b48a" :question/math-academy-id "q-132198" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $Y∼\\text{Exp}(\\pi ),$ calculate $P(1\\le Y\\le 3).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "An exponential random variable $Y$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda y}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nY∼\\text{Exp}(\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSo, $Y$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\pi {e}^{-\\pi y}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(1\\le Y\\le 3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le Y\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\pi {e}^{-\\pi y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\pi y}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3\\pi }+{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\  & ≈0.0431\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590757 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f9c704e-877c-5e19-a553-71eaa0db23b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590337} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f15f5b-2edc-51cf-9820-892edb82ffd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590475} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d29bbc-3676-5afd-8efd-bd666bfd90b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6565" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590491} {:answer/id #uuid "d8022a2c-701e-5a46-bd27-6bc97c855e12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6605" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590558} {:answer/id #uuid "e940a86f-7435-5a97-9ecd-47f0bad60632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590475} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e8cf8bc-9194-5649-8709-cd4b56704e2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11d6e15a-f482-5ee9-a082-96558ce44e34" :question/math-academy-id "q-132187" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right),$ calculate $P(X>3).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-x/6}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X>3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-x/6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-x/6}|}_{3}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.6065\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25a77678-46c9-5db7-8ec9-582308885b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7364" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590250} {:answer/id #uuid "2c0d07df-abc3-544b-b73d-a540c62b65df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6321" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590265} {:answer/id #uuid "464ada53-9a54-5fe5-99a4-666ebdee8d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9502" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590314} {:answer/id #uuid "64b8a84c-8529-50eb-8d85-32629e1d5c9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4866" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590360} {:answer/id #uuid "e546fc4a-0364-5cd9-a9c9-f7956ef026a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590582}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590250} :answer-field/id #uuid "bad16b3f-05f9-5cdf-b44d-ca689361f341" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3aa38dfc-ed32-536a-9b6a-6cebc1f209fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-96404" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right),$ calculate $P(X<2).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-2x/3}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X<2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-2x/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x/3}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4/3}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.7364\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074c9807-829a-5b98-88e6-e22b5c45096c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0302" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590199} {:answer/id #uuid "999a539f-f5b2-53fc-a3fb-1af00d3d0570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0498" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590442} {:answer/id #uuid "da8ed7c8-7b2e-51c0-b7e1-c140e9888c0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0137" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590566} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f0e059-8023-5f53-9e34-f9efa6044496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0357" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590588} {:answer/id #uuid "ed196434-71ad-5223-ab71-d1bb4bae5e66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590604}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590604} :answer-field/id #uuid "b945a8a9-ed36-5138-8d61-d35f93f2715b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "59d51f6d-7542-584a-991a-8702a2c9bfeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-96414" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right),$ calculate $P(X>11).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x/3}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X>11)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>11) & ={\\int }_{11}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{11}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/3}{|}_{11}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-11/3} \\\\  & ≈0.0256\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eadaea8-e7b3-5686-b233-7b20ab0a3079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1937" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590207} {:answer/id #uuid "26d24e13-8cc0-54e2-856a-ff9c409d1e4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1873" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590254} {:answer/id #uuid "74443e3f-bd90-55e0-a6f9-fdc76edc60e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1337" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590382} {:answer/id #uuid "81a5de1b-481c-5f2a-838a-35febcc380e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1984" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590399} {:answer/id #uuid "c95d8814-e1a3-54ed-b4b5-0af395479ea5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2077" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590207} :answer-field/id #uuid "47583c9c-029f-521c-88ed-ca428d7df852" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6bef3467-0f78-59bc-8533-35b28f402887" :question/math-academy-id "q-132205" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(0.8),$ calculate $P(2<X\\le 6).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(0.8).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.8{e}^{-0.8x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(2<X\\le 6)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X\\le 6) & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}0.8{e}^{-0.8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-0.8x}{|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4.8}+{e}^{-1.6} \\\\  & ≈0.1937\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590794 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fca2e6b-c0fc-54c6-9103-fd7806a0e109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1170" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590274} {:answer/id #uuid "68f0b13b-980e-5bb4-acd7-2d4c2d01155e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0855" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590365} {:answer/id #uuid "711f2c0f-8d35-51b8-94f7-dd4a0f26ea8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0752" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590377} {:answer/id #uuid "bea4ce14-e185-5a34-94b3-e751f9e3d1c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3496" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590509} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd3fa9a-2f69-5233-a209-14a8b6a969cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3181" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590531}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590365} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf7ccc5a-cc8b-5ab2-a236-f33a76c70d7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "72f86263-9eea-5221-aa9c-3f767d46f4f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-96424" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(4),$ calculate $P(\\frac{1}{2}<X<\\frac{3}{4}).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(4).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{-4x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(\\frac{1}{2}<X<\\frac{3}{4})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{2}<X<\\frac{3}{4}) & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{3/4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{3/4}4{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4x}{|}_{1/2}^{3/4} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3}+{e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.0855\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b0a7bd6-03ae-521b-8545-da3409ed32e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1632" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590223} {:answer/id #uuid "67f667e5-803c-5376-a9ee-3af7b687988d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590363} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c07159-94df-5828-b804-d2d556907b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1687" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590494} {:answer/id #uuid "b9abd58d-85f0-57dd-ba01-ff9802c19c07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1286" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590503} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ea624a-e6c2-5c01-b9ac-491a50134fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1485" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590585}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590503} :answer-field/id #uuid "65b72b22-371c-59d4-a214-caff24124a22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a388a466-adf5-55c1-86c8-9b03458b02ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-96438" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(5),$ calculate $P(\\frac{2}{5}<X\\le 1).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(5).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-5x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(\\frac{2}{5}<X\\le 1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{2}{5}<X\\le 1) & ={\\int }_{2/5}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2/5}^{1}5{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-5x}{|}_{2/5}^{1} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-5}+{e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.1286\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590803 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2597643f-a67e-54ef-b18b-2160e0cce9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5528" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590248} {:answer/id #uuid "48b3dc21-5e9e-5ac3-a283-1543db21b740" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5413" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590320} {:answer/id #uuid "4a23e0d5-9f92-5158-ba3d-4513ff625b2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4782" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590322} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb3aedd-180e-5af2-9a2b-5921c068ac1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4923" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590334} {:answer/id #uuid "d8118894-2c53-5000-9f2b-654d9bf5a00a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4993" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590559}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590334} :answer-field/id #uuid "b50d1ac3-a183-5666-9c76-8a1cee3a854b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a4b006e1-3691-563a-827d-de4c1c4be95e" :question/math-academy-id "q-96419" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $W∼\\text{Exp}(0.25),$ calculate $P(1\\le W\\le 5).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "An exponential random variable $W$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda w}, & w\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nW∼\\text{Exp}(0.25).\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.25{e}^{-0.25w}, & w\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(1\\le W\\le 5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le W\\le 5) & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f(w)\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}0.25{e}^{-0.25w}\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & =-{e}^{-0.25w}{|}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1.25}+{e}^{-0.25} \\\\  & ≈0.4923\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e61d92-68bc-5a2c-8cc3-04dff2b49bd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9502" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590195} {:answer/id #uuid "06842768-36ea-5ed2-a2e0-fa0c822afe3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9555" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590197} {:answer/id #uuid "23cca45d-2a54-552c-b14d-a4c26e98313a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9525" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590242} {:answer/id #uuid "4e51a5ad-b156-5923-b11e-0ab8a5e2989e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9052" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590332} {:answer/id #uuid "eca57a6c-8227-560c-bbd3-74666ac6b788" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9205" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590603}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590195} :answer-field/id #uuid "2813bc03-32db-5e77-aae3-8499a586dce0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac14f2a6-604c-5d8c-9597-5e7be9ebfc8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-132176" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(3),$ calculate $P(X<1).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(3).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X<1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.9502\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590816 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d48529-bf46-5b98-b62d-2543a5cc1497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2231" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590215} {:answer/id #uuid "25a000c5-1a60-52de-a5d9-1c076ef26c71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0357" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590249} {:answer/id #uuid "37c0de2f-07d2-5c79-8359-192385e4784e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0695" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590286} {:answer/id #uuid "6e769c95-2f53-5f81-8222-95ee86476ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3247" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590374} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8eca74-3a05-52dd-872d-76e8d29c4900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1653" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590447}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590286} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac82ac44-2beb-5312-9990-f7e4896d2d61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cb3c3164-617a-5f99-ba4a-1abb3fe0842e" :question/math-academy-id "q-96409" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $Y∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right),$ calculate $P(Y\\ge 2).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "An exponential random variable $Y$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda y}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nY∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $Y$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-4y/3}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(Y\\ge 2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 2) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-4y/3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{{e}^{-4y/3}|}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-8/3} \\\\  & ≈0.0695\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590819 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f7f74bb-4b00-544c-bc07-21bf4387972c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4668" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590299} {:answer/id #uuid "47f9585d-8dab-547c-aa0f-4df65652a7cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4066" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590319} {:answer/id #uuid "4edc3a0b-b3e2-53e6-83d4-f147efe22971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2366" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590335} {:answer/id #uuid "75d11e87-b5c8-50e6-8dd3-b60b1a0db49d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3525" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590384} {:answer/id #uuid "a68795c7-7cd6-54d6-93ee-a5777ff718c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4866" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590471}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590471} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f333aeb-e97a-524b-bfc5-d1b20aa2267d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ce1b241f-6f9c-54d8-be1f-868c5a0a0bb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-132168" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right),$ calculate $P(X\\le 2).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x/3}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X\\le 2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{{e}^{-x/3}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2/3}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.4866\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1856764a-5c2d-571d-b7d9-c3c6838b2f49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9999" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590220} {:answer/id #uuid "8b27fc07-3306-5587-8321-1af78f42afe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7534" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590417} {:answer/id #uuid "ba07ff3d-464f-584c-83c4-f77e26c13c37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8347" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590504} {:answer/id #uuid "cca3d597-0249-527f-80ec-3f2e3c030725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8647" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590533} {:answer/id #uuid "cf78f442-2b95-5066-b73a-b4f70859d19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7981" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590542} :answer-field/id #uuid "d083e6b6-e159-5313-9c77-b87edcfe74b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f6a28689-89f8-5372-b9bf-fe41dab67f62" :question/math-academy-id "q-96398" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $Y∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right),$ calculate $P(Y\\le 4).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "An exponential random variable $Y$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda y}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nY∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $Y$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{-2y/5}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(Y\\le 4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le 4) & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{-2y/5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2y/5}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-8/5}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.7981\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e999fe9-296c-5c45-ae03-39ed7b93e980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590228} {:answer/id #uuid "38f28e8b-e412-5e24-8a36-026808c74fa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0189" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590289} {:answer/id #uuid "596b1803-6b6d-550a-8797-9a49fd27208c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0011" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590351} {:answer/id #uuid "9faa9612-e01f-510c-ba89-0f5e212eb0dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590461} {:answer/id #uuid "ea79df63-f55d-5dd8-be2f-b7f63e837f8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0099" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590596}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590461} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4f372a0-1948-5872-9c02-c7188c470427" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ff9759da-993e-586a-b6fd-f929a219db2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-96340" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(3),$ calculate $P(X>2).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(3).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X>2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }3{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}{|}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-6} \\\\  & ≈0.0025\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f660532-8958-5f29-9061-06d637d2606d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591044} {:answer/id #uuid "29051936-f771-52d2-a3bf-d530a755fd26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}({y}^{2}-{y}^{3}), & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591065} {:answer/id #uuid "6081af46-82aa-5917-ad37-0cfe37b5272d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1+3{y}^{2}-4{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591161} {:answer/id #uuid "df1cc697-4378-5b9c-9435-c0127315949a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4(y-{y}^{3}), & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591425} {:answer/id #uuid "e712e8f7-3603-5cf7-9b52-df24d373ee31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591443}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591161} :answer-field/id #uuid "74fa8fb4-8d3c-5f91-9bd6-d9d675ae6767" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "028a6d78-101a-542f-9ff4-83c18779ade1" :question/math-academy-id "q-142709" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with a joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\,0\\le y\\le x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x\\le 1\\}.$\n\n![](images/2c/2cc3b27a1f926e9f5b5fdf59a5ea01fb7e28da53c7fc01535cc590f4c315258c.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{y}^{1}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{y}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=y}^{x=1} \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}+3x{y}^{2}]}_{x=y}^{x=1} \\\\  & =[({1}^{3}+3\\cdot 1\\cdot {y}^{2})-({y}^{3}+3\\cdot y\\cdot {y}^{2})] \\\\  & =1+3{y}^{2}-4{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1+3{y}^{2}-4{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e694d8a-0ed8-593a-b1c7-7cbe935ce0d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}, & -1\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591042} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd545bc-dca8-5d4f-9bec-a1f3a997457b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(1-{y}^{2})}{4}, & -1\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591094} {:answer/id #uuid "3dfc46fc-2e54-5c6e-87c4-e3badf361e9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(1+2{y}^{2})}{10}, & -1\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591097} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7bfacb-d860-5171-9553-35d0480f67a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{2}, & -1\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591126} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7e61b2-9de6-5f3b-8d3d-1a6fa57d5ded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(1+{y}^{2})}{8}, & -1\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591192}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591094} :answer-field/id #uuid "6340c500-3169-5387-add0-b3a3e520080f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "13e0ad0c-a8ea-52dc-bc79-837fed5333d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-142855" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with a joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{3x}{2({y}^{2}+1)},\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 1\\}.$\n\n![](images/cf/cf0c8438253a5b46a468610ca62bc17a9ce927c98045eb461291d98644241e2f.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $-1\\le y\\le 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{1}\\frac{3x}{2({y}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2({y}^{2}+1)}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2({y}^{2}+1)}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{x={y}^{2}}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4({y}^{2}+1)}[1-{({y}^{2})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4({y}^{2}+1)}[1-{y}^{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1-{y}^{2})(1+{y}^{2})}{4({y}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1-{y}^{2})}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(1-{y}^{2})}{4}, & -1\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591633 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d27e6fd-e52f-5424-bff1-96bc9444dad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{(y+6)^{2}}, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591151} {:answer/id #uuid "903cdaf1-65c7-554e-a752-3d4b572e5bb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3y}, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591261} {:answer/id #uuid "a83127f1-9325-502c-9eaa-75ccb9b6e152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3y}, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591309} {:answer/id #uuid "d4bf7ec9-50f8-5040-bef5-6ee590525718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3y}, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591403} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a95583-b25b-55c6-9d05-cb74ea61ac86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{(y+3)^{2}}, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591466}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591403} :answer-field/id #uuid "08c0c73b-a3be-5060-822b-fa7f585aedbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1e5625d5-5fb1-5fe9-b7bc-beb0b0db6f82" :question/math-academy-id "q-142886" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(\\frac{x}{3}+3y)}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $y\\ge 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(\\frac{x}{3}+3y)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-3{e}^{-(\\frac{x}{3}+3y)}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\infty } \\\\  & =-3{[{e}^{-(\\frac{x}{3}+3y)}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\infty } \\\\  & =-3[0-{e}^{-(0+3y)}] \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3y}, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ee23e0-1f6e-55d3-b420-8cff311cd4c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591021} {:answer/id #uuid "52af213b-b975-5c58-b9a0-cbe42e6abc6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591138} {:answer/id #uuid "7431034d-452b-5500-9a75-f71635f3f064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591205} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b8e6b5-d4e0-5fdf-8614-ea84d3460d98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591346} {:answer/id #uuid "f595435b-4338-5606-86ca-587ae3286de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591464}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591021} :answer-field/id #uuid "5658459b-9aa6-56a2-ac50-a558d66d4172" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2de97f32-f7ad-51d2-a68d-f92f1f60ace0" :question/math-academy-id "q-142968" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}\\,{x}^{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(Y\\le 1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{6}\\,{x}^{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}[{2}^{4}-{0}^{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}, & 0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le 1) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,{y}^{1/3}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{2}}{[\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{4/3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{2}}[{1}^{4/3}-{0}^{4/3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591656 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e158140-3603-5db6-8c28-4a913568f9bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y+{y}^{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591073} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba2c84a-a25a-50cc-b983-7c6a18504685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y+{y}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591125} {:answer/id #uuid "59c20bd9-ea92-5f55-a024-b7939dac47ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591144} {:answer/id #uuid "7649f8e6-a672-54e6-81be-5eabecc3c39e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591215} {:answer/id #uuid "f14848c8-e92d-56f4-82f0-cae23d722f47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591458}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591125} :answer-field/id #uuid "d201eafd-3c73-5e6c-8622-8fba14df71d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5488f7ef-a3fd-5433-bb0f-0f8a2a61219e" :question/math-academy-id "q-142860" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{Y}(y)$ of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ g(y), & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(y)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+1\\cdot y-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{f}_{Y}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}(\\frac{1}{2}+u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{u}{2}+\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[y+{y}^{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{y+{y}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{Y}(y)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ \\frac{y+{y}^{2}}{2}, & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(y)=\\frac{y+{y}^{2}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0581138f-51cc-5a1d-9413-579deb1fe20d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590999} {:answer/id #uuid "2005314e-3e53-5a62-8cca-153d2ca579da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591046} {:answer/id #uuid "a441f171-ecf1-5e8c-b306-0009b6e6be19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591300} {:answer/id #uuid "ac576111-2b15-5232-aec6-14a7eb8d58e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591321} {:answer/id #uuid "b82aa5b8-8a81-5fee-87d6-d83f04553030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591344}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591046} :answer-field/id #uuid "847373ee-a712-5b7e-b636-a8f2b82fd36f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6aaa2752-910d-5bf7-ab5e-6b65c9777a8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-142952" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(-3<Y<1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y, & 0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-3<Y<1) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591671 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17269eb6-d861-5905-b550-afd510d44738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2}, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591033} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd7038e-908b-5db6-bbe7-ebbc6457c543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591227} {:answer/id #uuid "81df4c3b-855b-560a-b12e-3aeeed81c62f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591240} {:answer/id #uuid "da972315-2dc0-5268-8653-8dd6ee248988" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8x}{{\\pi }^{2}}, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591411} {:answer/id #uuid "f0216f9c-d2d2-53a9-a1dd-0e8b1ba7edc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\pi }, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591456}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591456} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddaec570-8642-5daf-9e3d-28c29374ea54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "86994f22-4ed6-5278-9ec3-0eb9133c9cc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-142751" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{X}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\pi }, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591672 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0823bf41-1e23-5446-bbca-3abcf14cac9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591003} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc12e1b-0eb7-5ada-97a9-e6451066cd54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591009} {:answer/id #uuid "c244b21b-8d18-59f2-a2d0-7d1f7be6e49b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591359} {:answer/id #uuid "c8951a9b-3dcf-5839-bf57-9c01b2693a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591371} {:answer/id #uuid "d31de738-4883-5d11-b63e-848ae353af6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591400}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591400} :answer-field/id #uuid "8be69e6e-ba68-5e57-af44-2e5ad3ae65b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "87388207-1c0b-5684-a29e-8f08de85119d" :question/math-academy-id "q-142922" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(x+y)}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ g(x), & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $x\\ge 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(x+y)}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[-{e}^{-y}]}_{y=0}^{y=b} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }(1-{e}^{-b}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $X$ for $x\\ge 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}{f}_{X}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-u}]}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{X}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ 1-{e}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(x)=1-{e}^{-x}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013de305-55b7-5387-a63a-9d940b2ea75c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12x-3{x}^{2}}{32}, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590993} {:answer/id #uuid "04bb955f-dfbb-522a-b5e0-4492bca83534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4x, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186590998} {:answer/id #uuid "44a3270a-11f8-5fe5-9ac3-3bb906e4ad16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{64}, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591106} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac15e3d-c009-5eaf-8a80-5661e0baa77e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{64}, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591149} {:answer/id #uuid "f87e5d8b-2113-51b7-ac73-aa1dd7995578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{8}, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591469}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591149} :answer-field/id #uuid "56e1e657-8636-5d3e-b530-69b144cb4fc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a637704d-0464-5714-971d-5226ad40baf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-142705" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with a joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{16}x{y}^{3},\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{X}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}.$\n\n![](images/0a/0a3aeba14383b64a0414f3163828d972102073dc972d64105e779b317b8574b0.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{16}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{[\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}{[x{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}[x\\cdot (\\sqrt{x})^{4}-x\\cdot (\\sqrt{0})^{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}[x\\cdot {x}^{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{64}, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60b4f028-32d8-5094-b307-0a50f8cb7ceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2y}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591163} {:answer/id #uuid "6a318147-1015-5649-ab4b-7c825a45584e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591178} {:answer/id #uuid "7499674f-9b43-50b3-b996-9e56331234ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591208} {:answer/id #uuid "8f281d04-4bd7-5efd-9076-ba8aaac2a9c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y+{y}^{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591259} {:answer/id #uuid "d2ad103f-5db5-5522-97c9-137a9f1fd82c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+{y}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591398}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591259} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea389dff-fcf6-5fe4-a3d9-ec36ca89189a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "aec15b91-592c-5e0b-b3bc-4963d4e01bf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-142918" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{Y}(y)$ of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ g(y), & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(y)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}+1\\cdot {y}^{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{f}_{Y}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3{u}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{u}{2}+\\frac{{u}^{3}}{2}]}_{0}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[y+{y}^{3}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{y+{y}^{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{Y}(y)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ \\frac{y+{y}^{3}}{2}, & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(y)=\\frac{y+{y}^{3}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10464ab2-fb5b-5a27-b3c0-d5da52b5b85f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591018} {:answer/id #uuid "131b86c3-64bc-5d0e-b27e-8edd5885889c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591024} {:answer/id #uuid "5f2aa958-8b4f-500c-ad33-f49cf4b3632b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591157} {:answer/id #uuid "6df7f1e5-0147-542f-82b2-7e80cf7c4081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591187} {:answer/id #uuid "718eb0c3-e7b1-5092-b6f8-8c46ca1ddf8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591198}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591024} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a2d94b0-cc55-5460-9da7-aed70aadded3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cc02d07e-91fb-5d5d-b234-451aff2335b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-142963" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(Y\\ge 1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{16}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+x{y}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}[\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}+(2){y}^{3}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}(4+2{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 1) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}y+\\frac{1}{32}{y}^{4}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}(2)+\\frac{1}{32}{2}^{4}]-[\\frac{1}{4}(1)+\\frac{1}{32}{1}^{4}] \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591702 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e51a0b0-97b0-5bf0-a633-bb5af6c3248a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-\\sqrt{2}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y), & 0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591074} {:answer/id #uuid "6a5826c4-7e27-5b49-8d65-e6b5a9b8ae4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-\\sqrt{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y), & 0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591179} {:answer/id #uuid "7276a99b-1279-57ff-8763-d738c8ec9009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-\\sqrt{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+y)), & 0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591201} {:answer/id #uuid "b2dcd27e-80d6-5b5f-89a7-5a47a23e34f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-\\sqrt{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y), & 0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591335} {:answer/id #uuid "c9c80125-7c32-5258-9f19-4772a48d72ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-\\sqrt{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y), & 0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591372}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591074} :answer-field/id #uuid "626d38b7-310d-5999-9efe-0c0f771987f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cdab03ca-34df-52b0-ae2d-f7886faf7822" :question/math-academy-id "q-142870" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-\\sqrt{2}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-\\sqrt{2}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+y)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-\\sqrt{2}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-\\sqrt{2}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y), & 0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591710 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47dd38cd-f4dd-52ac-acc2-70e646995c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591119} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0c93b3-2cf8-5a3a-bc26-b75123baa542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591150} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2203e1-9cf0-50b4-be11-c633e59c7043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591186} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b6a0b3-dc09-5f5d-983f-b811a89a4e72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591331} {:answer/id #uuid "f673770c-78d1-5248-b5b5-20ff4c341d47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591465}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591150} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd05c2aa-178d-5e44-9ce2-49a21cc2e199" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d57a0a41-e072-545b-a61f-1f77ea6bd4df" :question/math-academy-id "q-142815" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(x+y)}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{X}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $x\\ge 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(x+y)}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-(x+y)}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\infty } \\\\  & =(0+{e}^{-(x+0)}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b83a274-e40a-5caf-b1be-2050dbbe83b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591068} {:answer/id #uuid "a3532a1c-b62b-5a8f-a000-a57689b6bd61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1024}{3125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591299} {:answer/id #uuid "b1cce001-3ab9-5259-a908-7154bbf71e2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591333} {:answer/id #uuid "c9dfa5fc-4a46-523c-ad3a-b0d95411f328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{512}{625}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591374} {:answer/id #uuid "e483623f-4cc4-5c42-908e-de8459032fc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591436}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591299} :answer-field/id #uuid "186439d2-8348-5d10-9b7c-8188bde62817" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d8d6522f-8b1d-5640-ad93-001b41be1c63" :question/math-academy-id "q-142964" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{3}x{y}^{4}, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(Y<\\frac{4}{5}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{10}{3}x{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}{y}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{y}^{4}({2}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =5{y}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{4}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<\\frac{4}{5}) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{4/5}{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/5}5{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{y}^{5}]}_{0}^{4/5} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{5}-{0}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1024}{3125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "642e6dbc-2b18-5046-9ed6-df562e24d04e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591167} {:answer/id #uuid "67dc7b49-5209-5539-8eba-5f408a27eb4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591175} {:answer/id #uuid "7394be1f-7525-588b-8367-8f5d6f2c9618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591203} {:answer/id #uuid "f6db558a-cdb9-5fba-a9a8-27291228e77d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591467} {:answer/id #uuid "f9861006-70e3-5894-97e9-1daa1bab6c91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591472}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591167} :answer-field/id #uuid "803b6850-6bb6-5334-a4e6-96a04a959f1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "eb2fe53f-84a4-58ab-aeed-b96d7ecf1f41" :question/math-academy-id "q-142920" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ g(x), & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $x\\ge 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}{[{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}({1}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $X$ for $x\\ge 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}{f}_{X}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{X}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{x}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(x)=1-\\frac{1}{x}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591723 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29949efc-8bfd-5125-8a20-50777755e5d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}, & y\\le 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591066} {:answer/id #uuid "754f6f06-79a7-5bd4-a09d-d2d8e3b0aacb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y{e}^{y}, & y\\le 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591211} {:answer/id #uuid "c691cad4-3e45-55e1-8b29-23315dce06e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591367} {:answer/id #uuid "d6378e8c-3e24-5c52-af39-8009c04e33bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{e}^{y}, & y\\le 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591407} {:answer/id #uuid "df8235e9-bd00-56a2-a243-ef0649886f8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-y{e}^{y}, & y\\le 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591427}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591427} :answer-field/id #uuid "522c1bec-5c6d-59af-a36b-240c4216ddec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186591528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f54fcb3c-960b-5614-830e-4646b7887fac" :question/math-academy-id "q-142708" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with a joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x{e}^{y},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\le -{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\le -{x}^{2}\\}.$\n\n![](images/1b/1bc2723ca6b5ee5af27bb6f9cb8e3462dde99a4686542b3f8c273a4148ede595.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $y\\le 0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{-y}}2x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{-y}}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{y}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{-y}} \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}{e}^{y}]_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{-y}} \\\\  & =[{(\\sqrt{-y})}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{y}-{0}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{y}] \\\\  & =-y{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-y{e}^{y}, & y\\le 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "151fc2f9-1161-5067-83c7-651eb6727fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592394} {:answer/id #uuid "88fb6401-77cb-57bd-b70b-8a0565dae19e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592704} {:answer/id #uuid "9d094f34-b1ac-5b6c-8f5a-446bdbd9393f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.02" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592754} {:answer/id #uuid "c930d0eb-d21a-5704-a885-50796d69fd57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592888} {:answer/id #uuid "e5fde62a-fb06-59e2-a848-86804746e793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592888} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa157cdb-496f-55c4-a7a1-b142f4101504" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "01cd9fca-2193-5783-80f6-562ef87e8b29" :question/math-academy-id "q-97203" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.92),$ compute $P(X=0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.92),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.92, & x=1 \\\\ 0.08, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=0)=f(0)=0.08.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f5b1f9-63fe-5b0c-a4b1-67fd3bbebe0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592413} {:answer/id #uuid "bea04e58-cc02-579b-bb83-a130aac5080a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592847} {:answer/id #uuid "c4798f36-7a13-5e3a-ba2f-c1a4c2326c56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592866} {:answer/id #uuid "cbad4152-ce5d-5a43-85f8-de488e9940d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592893} {:answer/id #uuid "e45d1b5d-2a7f-5981-aa46-5f4a466a9430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592951}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592847} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a9e9099-0455-5f30-9c88-6b5517ad432d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1f97ee58-0c1a-56c2-b925-34b652d5a854" :question/math-academy-id "q-170658" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right),$ compute $P(X=1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{6}, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=f(1)=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1be50d72-4f86-5bec-b6ab-95c332e5f555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592419} {:answer/id #uuid "763e9cbc-c3e4-5da1-b7c6-71374d9841ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592659} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab93143-801e-5c38-a865-f2cacf4eb482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592675} {:answer/id #uuid "b4cf93fc-b12d-578f-a79c-b210a39825cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592818} {:answer/id #uuid "f45a0d83-6c22-5087-9d1a-8f56b6fe189a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592987}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592987} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d91ec1-b73d-545d-b047-f60aeaec88a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2d427a27-c68f-54ea-be5a-f488ce63d40e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97196" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.1),$ compute $P(X=0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.1),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.1, & x=1 \\\\ 0.9, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=0)=f(0)=0.9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24a27841-80f2-5035-bd72-485564e9257a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592449} {:answer/id #uuid "58cd7738-0f34-5c17-b16f-4fa882f9dfe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592585} {:answer/id #uuid "626e1f8f-b878-527b-960c-a8ed644722ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592606} {:answer/id #uuid "9608139f-2646-5667-a9d4-c69918dbcfaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592739} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e391fb-a9bd-5cea-a6f2-648035b90f46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592783}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592606} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb77ce1c-ce3a-5f77-ac78-55b858b2d476" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4b1fe355-be21-55d9-88e9-b15f4870021b" :question/math-academy-id "q-170660" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right),$ compute $P(X=0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=0)=f(0)=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15b3bf2f-573a-5a2d-b5f9-877b9e867fd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4e+3{e}^{2}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592398} {:answer/id #uuid "24e81381-c025-5a0e-ad54-45662fb93995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2e-{e}^{2}+3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592451} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa3b55d-56b5-56d7-a689-35998c1523a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3e+{e}^{2}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592602} {:answer/id #uuid "7b431557-726d-5650-857f-3f44ab22ec48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4e-{e}^{2}-3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592676} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d718ee-5531-5fe1-a8e7-38e5c2012257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2e-{e}^{2}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592986}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592676} :answer-field/id #uuid "164b1296-a274-5d55-bda8-a478f091facf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6aa093db-db1c-5604-b14d-4beb6280682c" :question/math-academy-id "q-131155" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=e-1,$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x},\\, & 1\\le x\\le e, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}-{(e-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4e-{e}^{2}-3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593265 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021a66f9-15d6-5911-87e0-88a923aef8dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592348} {:answer/id #uuid "25d2221c-890c-50ad-9b8c-cc3571382496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592453} {:answer/id #uuid "591d02ba-beca-5baf-9b4b-20df2a41dc57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592586} {:answer/id #uuid "aa6bd597-c98d-5e01-a64b-0324890a5a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592786} {:answer/id #uuid "cbdc00bd-803b-5cc2-bea7-3832cd0cd9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592895}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592586} :answer-field/id #uuid "297eaaeb-bef5-52cb-9b2f-6e8daa4b739f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "86bdba3d-a920-587d-a794-19f8e2169813" :question/math-academy-id "q-97211" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.77),$ compute $P(X=1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.77),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.77, & x=1 \\\\ 0.23, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=f(1)=0.77.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593270 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39945687-5b53-5847-83e6-767056c6c879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592500} {:answer/id #uuid "5d09e065-4549-5f08-80ea-23f4c39df5c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592598} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0a626b-1df6-5059-b92e-baf5edbba245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592789} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a22b57-9710-5a28-aba2-ec0a21541745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592918} {:answer/id #uuid "d83653f9-9512-55b2-905a-815bc4b4c0c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592926}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592789} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f829451-f1a0-5bb7-94b2-4ffd90e9df42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f19ac6c-0f9e-5d34-9636-41cc02e7cdae" :question/math-academy-id "q-97210" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.16),$ compute $P(X=1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.16),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.16, & x=1 \\\\ 0.84, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=f(1)=0.16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13387953-67e5-55db-9603-96b07c7cd7a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592388} {:answer/id #uuid "557a5d01-b686-5762-84c3-cb2c23bd7a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592578} {:answer/id #uuid "71b70c1d-b868-5567-a6c4-e1f0b925a099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592652} {:answer/id #uuid "8773a336-582e-58bc-8d2e-c92de652d5a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592700} {:answer/id #uuid "9e82eeb7-4107-504b-8289-d2d267f7f7c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592761}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592700} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4d62433-669f-5529-85c5-6c6adcc89e8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "99b7d013-003d-567d-9a25-4182e399709f" :question/math-academy-id "q-90546" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11}{12},$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}, & 0<x<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}, & 1\\le x<3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot (1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{1}+{\\frac{{x}^{3}}{18}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{71}{45}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{71}{45}-{\\left(\\frac{11}{12}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.74\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b1b4ace-2d2e-5575-8f29-4d218218e5d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592417} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc35f73-5a46-5afd-8de9-929e9dc222cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592478} {:answer/id #uuid "c292f812-69a7-5409-863c-0a74d4d923ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592859} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf2394d-096f-56d8-925c-8055f33c5b4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592897} {:answer/id #uuid "ff16632f-51d7-56df-85ae-de9a52e6358e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593009}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593009} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6674f1c-d6fd-53c9-afc6-59ef65dbd632" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d21b6dab-24ae-5e48-bbf3-ff8f673bb44d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97198" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.27),$ compute $P(X=0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.27),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.27, & x=1 \\\\ 0.73, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=0)=f(0)=0.73.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d667f78-61c9-5555-bd29-bbd4dd5c1d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592427} {:answer/id #uuid "3f12a019-d0bc-53fd-a3ee-07950701f8e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592522} {:answer/id #uuid "46db2d0b-d619-5d1f-bdfe-7c789c4dc6a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592542} {:answer/id #uuid "545c2ae1-2de0-5986-8bd6-3621a26b3fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592575} {:answer/id #uuid "bf56385f-6061-57a8-96ae-6b424bb545c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592849}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592427} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e14a7dc-2ea8-5b00-83b2-0050fab662b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d937d2b0-bac8-5667-bce8-66417e1c6307" :question/math-academy-id "q-97202" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.87),$ compute $P(X=0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.87),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.87, & x=1 \\\\ 0.13, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=0)=f(0)=0.13.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593304 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab6e5a8-3afd-59ec-ab02-3b988b830b73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592463} {:answer/id #uuid "b53a641f-4a0c-5f8b-b8b3-e4a3d94cca01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592819} {:answer/id #uuid "ca854eab-bb07-5157-a66a-04eebe4c8b76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592890} {:answer/id #uuid "eaccafe1-80a9-5727-a738-47aeb0860282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592968} {:answer/id #uuid "f1eada11-ec2f-5376-a15a-3278b58f4bff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186592981}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592819} :answer-field/id #uuid "19b37d70-aba7-59d0-bec2-ea2fe2d82e8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fa05d63d-6128-5215-b7df-8cfe25e84f32" :question/math-academy-id "q-170657" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right),$ compute $P(X=1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=f(1)=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13be2041-38a5-5f52-b1e5-c2dd9f95ffe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593426} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5eea8a-8ecb-5cca-8c74-1a41dbd9d72c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593510} {:answer/id #uuid "c4743ba4-c2ba-5031-a6bb-f992f27204c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593851} {:answer/id #uuid "dbec9359-c558-5957-8e9c-e9a37478b78f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593901} {:answer/id #uuid "ea3f2b3d-3cdf-5df3-923b-eba617aa2b34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593931}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593931} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fa636b3-f5de-5d3e-a215-f00fd104d1e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0dbc51ee-15ed-52a0-a21b-3201565510a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-145119" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(X<\\frac{2}{3}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x+y}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2+2y-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(y+1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(y+1)}{3}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 2,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{x+y}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2(y+1)}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{2(y+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{2(y+1)}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<\\frac{2}{3}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{2}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2/3}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2/3}\\frac{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}{2(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2/3}(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[{x}^{2}+x]}_{0}^{2/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{2}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\left(\\frac{10}{9}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49797651-d75d-5b8c-9edb-8de32546def2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593556} {:answer/id #uuid "78412886-0716-5e91-bbab-779c43a45715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593661} {:answer/id #uuid "90a054a7-5132-5cb9-84c7-749d6119541b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593725} {:answer/id #uuid "92cdf376-3dbe-5d00-88fc-fa0047956eab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593730} {:answer/id #uuid "bf771000-640b-5ba4-87c8-a0030264cb02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593834}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593834} :answer-field/id #uuid "63442e50-d2a4-543e-9976-d1c33887296b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "29a9c010-814a-5d5c-a115-d796744175b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-148350" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{11}{18},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.2582,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.2664,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot (4{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}xy)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{12}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}-\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{11}{18}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{108}\\right)}{(0.2582)\\cdot (0.2664)} \\\\  & ≈0.13,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "421233ee-c5e3-544a-8479-a4ffeb0830e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593536} {:answer/id #uuid "5dcdf948-d921-5d2f-941a-24cb93e94df7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-y}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593598} {:answer/id #uuid "817b8276-9838-522a-8c14-f181a8c53c7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593685} {:answer/id #uuid "8a975a63-ab05-5763-8427-541b7236f61d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593712} {:answer/id #uuid "f1fd7459-9620-52ab-a5d1-09d4175f500b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2x-y}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593951}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593685} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f60ca5-c9bf-5ef4-ac33-d1a0842269a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "32e245f7-b140-5c0a-bd45-81f7042158f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-143632" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2x-y}, & \\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-2x-y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{-y}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{-y}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }\\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2}\\right){|}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }(1-{e}^{-2b}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}, & \\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{-2x-y}}{{e}^{-y}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2x}, & \\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fdf8f1c-b84c-56c3-a31d-8d97eb95ba94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593451} {:answer/id #uuid "307b0591-6ac9-5028-96f6-3bd9c08df847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593491} {:answer/id #uuid "5c125c8a-9671-5e18-b65f-4fc4810dd6ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593596} {:answer/id #uuid "76168dee-4089-5ffb-a897-e3a994726170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593650} {:answer/id #uuid "8b28239a-75e4-53fe-b002-750b6f3347bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593713}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593491} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c695385-8f3c-53c1-abdd-94c78b29ed93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "48194873-0262-57b6-977e-d08b7a2391fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-148407" :question/problem "Maria collects stickers for a sticker album, where each sticker in the album has a different number on it.\nSo far, Maria has duplicates of the following sticker numbers:\n\n$$\n7,\\,12,\\,28,\\,35,\\,78,\\,81,\\,99,\\,112\n$$\n\nIf Maria picks a duplicate sticker at random to give to a friend, what is the probability that the sticker number she picks is greater than $70?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number on the chosen sticker. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{7,\\,12,\\,28,\\,35,\\,78,\\,81,\\,99,\\,112\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X>70).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{7,\\,12,\\,28,\\,35,\\,78,\\,81,\\,99,\\,112\\},$ and the set $S=\\{7,\\,12,\\,28,\\,35,\\,78,\\,81,\\,99,\\,112\\}$ has $|S|=8$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}, & x=7,\\,12,\\,28,\\,35,\\,78,\\,81,\\,99,\\,112 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X>70$ are $78,\\,81,\\,99,\\,112.$ There are $4$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X>70)=4\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{4}{8}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d264558-e46c-5e25-862e-d378733b12f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{65}{72}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593409} {:answer/id #uuid "6d89f0fb-6019-5d93-b25d-a61f33ad879c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{55}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593633} {:answer/id #uuid "7d59a7d7-5b61-506a-abac-1d1301608dd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{48}{72}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593675} {:answer/id #uuid "c20d28e3-c682-51b0-8361-a8a3c7b9c4ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{71}{72}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593846} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4feb9e-5305-50cb-9b3c-dfe22564f74e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593977}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593846} :answer-field/id #uuid "d80d9b05-3b0e-5d84-82c0-e1b4164f07e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "49c1fa94-41fa-54ea-b43b-1640e0c576cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-145109" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,0<x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{2}\\,x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<Y\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x.$ \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<Y\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{2}) & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{3\\sqrt{2}/2}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x=\\frac{3}{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{3\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{y}{{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}{\\int }_{1/4}^{3\\sqrt{2}/2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1/4}^{3\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}({\\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}(\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{71}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{71}{72}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b7d3da-98f0-59f8-a828-e6395cfabc5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593384} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec0150c-bcf5-586c-8b10-a71a89193876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593720} {:answer/id #uuid "979a0290-2039-5a43-b491-f1738717cc6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593744} {:answer/id #uuid "a422c5ed-1585-54e7-a8c4-0bcbd3cdd946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593773} {:answer/id #uuid "b225e6a3-a995-5e83-ad0f-c7acf820d683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593806}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593773} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d219dcd-6070-5a3e-b52a-77111a5d0112" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4e607124-453f-5d89-a238-255edf1e5015" :question/math-academy-id "q-148409" :question/problem "Jennifer's marbles are labeled with numbers $7$ through $22.$ If Jennifer chooses a marble at random, what is the probability that the marble number she picks is greater than or equal to $10$ and less than $17?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number on the chosen marble. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{7,8,…,22\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(10\\le X<17).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{7,8,…,22\\},$ and the set $S=\\{7,8,…,22\\}$ has $|S|=22-7+1=16$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}, & x=7,8,…,22 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $10\\le X<17$ are $10,11,12,13,14,15,16.$ There are $7$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(10\\le X<17)=7\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}=\\frac{7}{16}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594172 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20405660-6c0b-5ca7-ba39-49318f923999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593453} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5929b0-6a8d-5eea-9347-66bef6e2d72d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593508} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb31232-8837-522b-a7c6-407f4aa0219e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593761} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc26862-dacf-5bf0-b731-b91521b8f691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593867} {:answer/id #uuid "e982debc-1e70-59c5-92c8-3bf186bca349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593928}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593867} :answer-field/id #uuid "3571fd4f-168f-5e64-9935-badf075a69c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "63a61000-be39-5f78-93de-ca9b7047ca52" :question/math-academy-id "q-97652" :question/problem "Ann and Steven are playing a card game. Ann chooses a card at random from a deck of cards labeled $15$ through $40.$ If the number she picks is greater or equal to $20$ and less than $25,$ Ann wins, otherwise Steven wins. What is the probability that Ann wins the game?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the card number Ann picks. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{15,16,…,40\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(20\\le X<25).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{15,16,…,40\\},$ and the set $S=\\{15,16,…,40\\}$ has $|S|=40-15+1=26$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{26}, & x=15,16,…,40 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $20\\le X<25$ are $20,21,22,23,24.$ There are $5$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(20\\le X<25)=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}=\\frac{5}{26}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "570925b3-8f8e-5cfc-be3c-d11210838de1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x(-{x}^{2}+2)}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593587} {:answer/id #uuid "85e50e3b-a3bf-510f-93ad-4f3b74f6e5b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593700} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f96c07-df9e-5631-a1ab-5b08985ba963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x}{-{x}^{2}+x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593845} {:answer/id #uuid "e81823df-fe9a-5209-8d9a-f0d037ea3367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{-{x}^{2}+x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593926} {:answer/id #uuid "fc87f1c1-2bd7-5292-b412-77b27fcb26c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-{x}^{2}+2)}{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593974}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593926} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b48f238-da66-5ee8-a342-90bced86a287" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7e6e5bd8-8a05-549b-9cb9-4a44448da5e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143640" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{8}, & \\,0\\le x<2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0\\le x<2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}\\frac{3x}{8}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{8}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{8}\\cdot (x+2-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3x(-{x}^{2}+x+2)}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x(-{x}^{2}+x+2)}{8}, & \\,0\\le x<2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x<2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3x}{8}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3x(-{x}^{2}+x+2)}{8}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-{x}^{2}+x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{-{x}^{2}+x+2}, & \\,0\\le x<2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594191 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18903c8d-5af3-519e-ad20-4a7cefb6f46b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593436} {:answer/id #uuid "94f09c21-753a-58f6-af25-017332b8bc61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(xy+1)}{y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593736} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2a6ea1-c5cb-533f-8f0d-080814ac2153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy+1}{2(y+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593785} {:answer/id #uuid "c128e7b2-ce09-5b01-84a0-a50607ef5b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+y}{2(y+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593841} {:answer/id #uuid "cec16073-4f00-5b15-9764-b1607114d278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y+1}{xy+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593785} :answer-field/id #uuid "45ae3fb8-74fc-52d6-a846-bf0aaf975b50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "93b3b076-040f-57da-a9b0-481b207ab196" :question/math-academy-id "q-145095" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{xy+1}{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{xy+1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(xy+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2}+x]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [2y+2-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2(y+1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(y+1)}{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{xy+1}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2(y+1)}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy+1}{2(y+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{xy+1}{2(y+1)}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f7487bd-265d-5bc7-83cc-c9c0931145d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593486} {:answer/id #uuid "4a372f38-7a87-5ac9-8ff0-573c9721656e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4xy}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593558} {:answer/id #uuid "71270774-73ea-5328-b718-4fa3a01b74ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15xy}{1-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593642} {:answer/id #uuid "75bdfbd1-7c58-5d54-ac99-6fff965f30ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593647} {:answer/id #uuid "db46d93a-ea7d-50a7-8cb9-cabac0aefd46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}y}{15(1-{x}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593899}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593486} :answer-field/id #uuid "55e388c2-eef8-5ae9-a3d3-7c9872756e99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9bec54a3-d311-5351-a212-c493a73b9300" :question/math-academy-id "q-143615" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{1-{x}^{2}}, & 0\\le x<1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{2})}{2}, & 0\\le x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ and the marginal density function ${f}_{X}(x)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x<1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nFrom the equation above, it follows that\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\cdot {f}_{X}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x<1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{1-{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{15{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{2})}{2} \\\\  & =15{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ equals $0.$ As a result, the product will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the joint probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}15{x}^{2}y, & 0\\le x<1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3693820a-1870-5848-9030-438ce9172e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593501} {:answer/id #uuid "78acea98-d856-506b-97db-a44a12229297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593662} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae90186-9131-57b6-8399-82183c7ae35f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593753} {:answer/id #uuid "bba38901-beba-5400-9447-3315323caf6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593826} {:answer/id #uuid "fa257460-0d27-5b77-b2fe-d268692bba91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593968}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593662} :answer-field/id #uuid "52b233f1-7196-540f-9aaf-964ccf28135d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aeede78e-94df-54ad-9afa-3cff3ecafc80" :question/math-academy-id "q-145041" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+2y}{2y+1}, & \\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X<\\frac{3}{4}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(X\\in A\\,|\\,Y=y)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<\\frac{3}{4}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{2}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/4}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/4}\\frac{(2x+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})}{(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3/4}(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[{x}^{2}+x]_{0}^{3/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{9}{16}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{21}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23eae862-9580-5e02-be32-ce2dbd81aec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593464} {:answer/id #uuid "815ceb11-5177-5da1-aa23-e72cb20f85b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593684} {:answer/id #uuid "9b1f7e60-68fc-5873-a8e5-d18bda03e240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593756} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f211e1-9c03-50bc-bfaf-d124fe3d9f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593805} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2ae822-ff62-59bb-b286-0a52de7c81b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593937}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593937} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f9dabf5-ba1d-5337-90cd-b4c042cf5810" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ca549ea0-069f-517c-8dea-30a9de93b0a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-97628" :question/problem "In a math test, a student has to answer a multiple-choice question, and they decide to select an answer at random. The answers are labeled from $1$ to $5.$ What is the probability that the student picks one of the first $2$ answers?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the selected answer. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{1,2,…,5\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X\\le 2).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{1,2,…,5\\},$ and the set $S=\\{1,2,…,5\\}$ has $|S|=5-1+1=5$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}, & x=1,2,…,5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X\\le 2$ are $1,2.$ There are $2$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 2)=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1572b57a-dff2-5ebd-8871-f0e3a47e3677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593430} {:answer/id #uuid "4750a9d7-5626-52ae-b638-7bcd5c7c0cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593553} {:answer/id #uuid "9fb8933e-b886-5556-b4bb-aabbd9801367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593765} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c40c99-1fc1-5e4b-bc92-e97e3142de3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593949} {:answer/id #uuid "f44e224b-a122-56f4-9ab4-0388bd861372" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593949} :answer-field/id #uuid "be261ba0-d614-5a73-b85e-4435b032bf31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d1413b7f-f919-5052-8fef-4e7307d9f409" :question/math-academy-id "q-145127" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(x+2y)}{2}, & \\,0\\le y\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(X<\\frac{2}{5}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{5}).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{y}^{1}\\frac{3(x+2y)}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{y}^{1}(x+2y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+2xy]}_{x=y}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}+2y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\frac{1+4y-5{y}^{2}}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+4y-5{y}^{2})}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(1+4y-5{y}^{2})}{4}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le y\\le x\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3(x+2y)}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3(1+4y-5{y}^{2})}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+2y)}{1+4y-5{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(x+2y)}{1+4y-5{y}^{2}}, & \\,0\\le y\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<\\frac{2}{5}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{5}) & ={\\int }_{y}^{2/5}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{1}{5})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/5}^{2/5}\\frac{2(x+2\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right))}{(1+4\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)-5{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/5}^{2/5}\\frac{2(x+\\frac{2}{5})}{\\left(\\frac{8}{5}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1/5}^{2/5}(5x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}+2x]}_{1/5}^{2/5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{1}{10}-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a2c0631-0bd4-5d71-9836-be598891083b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593439} {:answer/id #uuid "2606abf0-8418-5f2c-ba74-449c818c1e05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593470} {:answer/id #uuid "a2873844-44cc-5aa7-bbd2-59778cdbb622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593770} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e952be-961b-57b8-9c32-60b303de06eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593819} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f815f9-bbd5-5fec-a212-23cc136e306d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593930}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593819} :answer-field/id #uuid "358713e9-f69b-5d1e-aa48-49a9e0be9a90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d96a62c4-445f-5a0f-be6a-6a822a55817d" :question/math-academy-id "q-148355" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+{e}^{y}}{e}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3e+1}{6e},\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{3}{2e},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.2821,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.2870,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2x+{e}^{y}}{e}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2}y+xy{e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y{e}^{y}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{2}{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}y{e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}y{e}^{y}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{y}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{e}{2}-\\frac{e}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6e}-\\left(\\frac{3e+1}{6e}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{2e}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{e-3}{12{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{\\left(\\frac{e-3}{12{e}^{2}}\\right)}{(0.2821)\\cdot (0.2870)} \\\\  & ≈-0.04,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "155ba72f-3d7d-58c5-993f-1188c36455e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593429} {:answer/id #uuid "30e28dec-fa23-5b7f-9951-fee0e5844de3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593492} {:answer/id #uuid "3920bbbd-260b-58f8-936a-7e75927e44a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593506} {:answer/id #uuid "7b592af0-5c74-5889-89a9-b3b159179d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593668} {:answer/id #uuid "bb54513e-d2d5-58b8-9412-15eed13734e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593824}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593668} :answer-field/id #uuid "5efc2d6a-f8ad-5667-a1ec-f91f444b3514" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "df202318-e036-5725-91a7-7b2e2e6925e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143624" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(X<Y\\,|\\,X=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}2x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x{[{e}^{y}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =2x(2-1) \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0<x<1,0<y<\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x{e}^{y}}{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<Y\\,|\\,X=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)) & =P(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)<Y\\,|\\,X=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3/2)}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right))\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3/2)}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{e}^{y}]}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3/2)}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d95be1-9570-5a00-a102-d264aff4e1f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593437} {:answer/id #uuid "49bac261-ae12-51ff-8c42-c331e9bce4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593557} {:answer/id #uuid "4e357132-43b8-5ae7-994c-39c13a13ac57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593569} {:answer/id #uuid "82ac5fe2-3210-5b78-abfa-235c935b281e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593689} {:answer/id #uuid "d6eaf161-1e68-52ab-b429-22846c9e03fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593886}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593557} :answer-field/id #uuid "e21b54d5-b641-576f-92cf-bde2d4c9f57c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e8e3e751-672b-5965-af1d-da39efcbadf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-148334" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{xy+2}{4}, & \\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{12},\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.5713,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.2887,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{xy+2}{4}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2xy)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{2}-(-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}-\\left(\\frac{1}{12}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{72}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{72}\\right)}{(0.5713)\\cdot (0.2887)} \\\\  & ≈0.08,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594234 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2adf7dc2-b121-5b2d-a09e-876fee16c458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1-{x}^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593481} {:answer/id #uuid "4c30ed16-d3fe-54a4-96d0-29778839ed94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{1-{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593561} {:answer/id #uuid "5676c57c-88a7-5e17-bad9-412cdebaa995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy(1-{x}^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593585} {:answer/id #uuid "7645cc41-db55-5ec2-b263-2eb627b7bd82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{2(1-{x}^{4})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593653} {:answer/id #uuid "9519fd2b-3db5-5c5e-b8fc-de56c98df6ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{1-{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593739}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593561} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b4583ae-8ad1-545d-860e-496b4f5437ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f4a2e6a7-5117-5150-b7b8-3b6ab1df9427" :question/math-academy-id "q-143639" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{21{x}^{2}y}{2}, & \\,-1<x\\le 0,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $-1<x\\le 0,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\frac{21{x}^{2}y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{21{x}^{2}}{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{21{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{{x}^{2}}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{21{x}^{2}}{4}\\cdot (1-{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{21{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{4})}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{21{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{4})}{4}, & \\,-1<x\\le 0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $-1<x\\le 0,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{21{x}^{2}y}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{21{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{4})}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{1-{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{1-{x}^{4}}, & \\,-1<x\\le 0,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b07b60-926e-5752-89ce-7b5a5eeb3269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593390} {:answer/id #uuid "46696891-07a8-5095-b53b-be56920d5ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593550} {:answer/id #uuid "8313a34d-eec8-52c5-b7ea-39138b4b0764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593692} {:answer/id #uuid "83c5dff4-3b6d-596b-9e14-62c53ed43b3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593693} {:answer/id #uuid "d90ab71c-e77d-5600-b445-255796ab89c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593891} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b3d5e0d-4556-56ec-aa2c-d0e79fbc28b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f4fcbf19-11d5-5c21-9516-e30e5bf38915" :question/math-academy-id "q-148404" :question/problem "Mike has a box with $7$ red LEGO blocks numbered from $1$ to $7$ and other $7$ blue LEGO blocks numbered from $8$ to $14.$ If Mike chooses a LEGO block at random, what is the probability that he picks a blue LEGO block?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number on the chosen LEGO block. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{1,2,…,14\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(8\\le X\\le 14).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{1,2,…,14\\},$ and the set $S$ has $|S|=14$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{14}, & x=1,2,…,14 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $8\\le X\\le 14$ are $8,9,10,11,12,13,14.$ There are $7$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(8\\le X\\le 14)=7\\cdot \\frac{1}{14}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fcfa4b7-c916-54c9-a953-58ca4ac1f19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593450} {:answer/id #uuid "656295f1-ebd3-58c2-bb8c-765344439ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}{3({y}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593612} {:answer/id #uuid "68afc157-3036-5831-9789-faf8c13fd136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1)}{{y}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593620} {:answer/id #uuid "dca54d9d-a1e2-512e-8148-62f293d15c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(xy+1)}{y+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593902} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4be62b-7ba9-5e60-84c4-1b7fda7634c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}(xy+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593969}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593620} :answer-field/id #uuid "69f8ff5d-95a8-5094-a7ea-98c2e2bfaffb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fcf706c6-7e10-5c3f-b0cf-d9c199f24a41" :question/math-academy-id "q-144945" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1)}{16}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3({y}^{2}+3)}{16}, & 1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ and the marginal density function ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{9({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1)}{16}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3({y}^{2}+3)}{16}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1)}{{y}^{2}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1)}{{y}^{2}+3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3df5fa9c-a5e1-508d-a181-de8c02678f82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=2$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594395} {:answer/id #uuid "544e3d16-e1ea-53ca-abbc-27231cee8114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594411} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff8ee0c-7102-53f1-8ec6-6a03f2d71611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594437} {:answer/id #uuid "90063b1a-c6dc-543d-9114-77f15982ded7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594471} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a7a5f6-97d0-5b52-a441-39da29be536b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594543}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594395} :answer-field/id #uuid "d386df52-1c9a-54d0-984e-a271d6b24d38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2c84f753-589b-5ce1-9459-1771a16344eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-36697" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+x & \\text{ for }\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x<\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=0$ from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡0 \\\\  & =2(1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ from the right, we have $x>\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6eea7866-5af5-5eac-9c7d-9570dbc715c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594434}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594434} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c359556-5f0b-5f99-b43e-22f9ba1df4d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "94c3f77e-19f9-57a5-ad10-3a09e905fba0" :question/math-academy-id "q-302093" :question/problem "Find the right-sided limit at $x=2$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-16\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2x+5}-\\sqrt{2x}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{2x+5}-\\sqrt{2x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(\\sqrt{2x+5}-\\sqrt{2x}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2\\cdot 2)+5}-\\sqrt{2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9}-\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594714 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d0147a7-0d5d-5c1f-a98f-5a4b9a122e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594416} {:answer/id #uuid "70114d54-278a-5b22-ae1f-646a78c427cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594438} {:answer/id #uuid "70e1033f-fcf9-593f-af72-a2c812371adb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594440} {:answer/id #uuid "e4964eca-7438-563e-9f11-6b1050519016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594561} {:answer/id #uuid "f21ac1e4-411c-5e95-a353-aea07881bbe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594575}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594561} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfe1c40e-f769-5a17-b233-2d2ba771bf66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ca8a3dd5-f4ee-5b3b-bff7-8a83e07ba125" :question/math-academy-id "q-80570" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1.$ If $R=[-1,1]\\times [-4,4],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}|{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}|\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R \\\\ |{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}|, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}|{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}|, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}|{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}|, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/3f/3fe53f71501bccf9bc2c6bb4144a63d9cf1614ef229b5ca3b640fcf130968fd2.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $|{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}|$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}|{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}|\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions II and III equal $|{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}|$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function I. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,4)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594725 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "619ba8f0-d522-5f2f-aa49-1bffc6592afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594425} {:answer/id #uuid "8343f068-8fce-5793-a987-e500e3eabd76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594460} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ff07c1-f8ed-5073-9c5d-a8e643b924fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594523} {:answer/id #uuid "c980e977-32a2-5e25-96a0-23346f43d712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594532} {:answer/id #uuid "f9926336-3411-5e84-9a3e-f513185b69cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594460} :answer-field/id #uuid "b865c934-bcca-57ae-a717-9b9291098d65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f1dac8e6-80ac-5844-8f29-38963e5758f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-86893" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=3-|x|.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,3]$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [1,3]$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [0,3]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/01/012c298cc2defbf82e240f266f94733cdcd8e579ad10535eb94f33becf528b8d.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is false. The rectangle $R$ does not completely cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "848708c2-cd82-52ca-afdf-1c3cdb652e1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595268} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4de34d-88b7-53e9-8f49-0ce3d770c328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595306} {:answer/id #uuid "925483b8-a0fa-5b5c-a802-cbafb77e5c8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595317} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ce1071-561b-5e38-bc6c-7f987e52eba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595662} {:answer/id #uuid "fd52bb59-3d76-5198-9b11-13e105e5f6e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595673}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595306} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7de609e-d993-5e3e-b4a4-15f698027135" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "04c9a043-611c-5f87-ba09-184f6bf9d6c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-143354" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 3).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,3]\\times [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & \\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2}{9}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{9}x[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{9}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)^{2}}{9}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595922 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3bad9e7-ba7b-5e7f-9ec4-8ee827e9bd0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595438} {:answer/id #uuid "d5eb5c54-d0cf-5285-837b-aef2413ff57d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595538} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b64ca1-e102-5eee-86bd-4b8736b438b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595545} {:answer/id #uuid "d8168b3d-0589-56ea-b13c-fdcc568bc169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595546} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c659c4-cf17-57f8-a959-83e8191a92b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595661}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595538} :answer-field/id #uuid "8feed906-a622-5b1f-8a45-11b12635aab8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0c28a44d-b3f9-5ffa-b220-06f365490293" :question/math-academy-id "q-143575" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{(x+1)^{2}}, & x\\ge 0,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expected value of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal density function for $Y$ when $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2y}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{(x+1)}]}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & =2y\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{b+1}) \\\\  & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot 2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0176aae3-fbd8-59e0-9df0-a3b64b394fb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}(1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594824} {:answer/id #uuid "42cb5eaa-97b1-5c1c-b2af-a9814494adfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}(1-\\frac{1}{e})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595033} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f13974-65fe-52a8-9d21-dab7f5133af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}(1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595378} {:answer/id #uuid "c15f75c2-a87d-5b36-a4b1-2d2502965c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}(1-\\frac{1}{e})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595478} {:answer/id #uuid "dd597b33-8f38-5674-81dd-eedae7a839b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595562}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594824} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccb7967e-0bc0-5ce0-99ae-3b3135ec07ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0f04e9c4-e396-5f9c-afd4-215de687a8b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-143353" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2x}, & \\,0\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}y, & \\,1\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 2).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y{e}^{-2x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,\\infty )\\times [1,3].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y{e}^{-2x}, & \\,0\\le x,\\,1\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{3{e}^{-2x}}{8}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{e}^{-2}}{8}-\\left(-\\frac{3}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f76594c-2130-5267-aee6-f5ce43abacb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}({x}^{2}-1){e}^{y}, & \\,2\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595242} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ee09f9-a9d5-505b-bd37-4ea359a7ef9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(x-1){e}^{y}, & \\,2\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595448} {:answer/id #uuid "b995666f-b7b4-5bdd-a2be-8001ac411be8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}(2x-1){e}^{y}, & \\,2\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595456} {:answer/id #uuid "f291c2bc-7091-5ef4-a2c0-cd42d5822d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using only the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595635} {:answer/id #uuid "f6fb5183-da01-53cb-8e78-f481f920886b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(y-1){e}^{x}, & \\,2\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595647}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595635} :answer-field/id #uuid "567aaa50-2b09-5fbe-a513-743444efe8bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "27c8be0c-65fe-5f8e-818d-93dfca5e16bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-143349" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}(x-1), & \\,2\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025e1656-6569-5f0c-9d35-4a9649c68ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594828} {:answer/id #uuid "14ee30f9-28fa-530a-8b4d-4c886737d5cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594893} {:answer/id #uuid "1740426b-af3f-5e9d-8369-e133f777e9ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594898} {:answer/id #uuid "22ae26c9-9596-5e48-b396-13624a4a9305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594938} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f8070c-ef96-5a04-af85-c1b407db7136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595646}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594893} :answer-field/id #uuid "99fc683c-02f9-5242-8b60-47d1be9066c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "315fb257-a450-57a3-a9b4-d26ab05eaa97" :question/math-academy-id "q-143385" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(1+xy),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{y+1}{3}$ for $y\\in [0,2]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}(1+xy)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[x+\\frac{y{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1+\\frac{y}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{y+2}{6},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,2].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}(1+xy)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[y+\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2+2x-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [0,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{1}{3}(1+xy)}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}\\ne {{\\left(\\frac{2(x+1)}{3}\\right)}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{\\left(\\frac{y+2}{6}\\right)}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is *not* true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements is true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8b9993-09f3-5cf6-88a6-32ffe73f8e9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594856} {:answer/id #uuid "88b140c8-a9a5-52fa-b11d-c916c6d28667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595282} {:answer/id #uuid "a92a52f2-d7bc-5f41-acb1-5fb8d8129d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595408} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4758c4-b39d-5c64-9d71-9707104665d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595418} {:answer/id #uuid "be390658-2eba-563e-878c-cc136e1c4d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595470}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595470} :answer-field/id #uuid "12866dfa-998d-5041-843f-9c40a816ca92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "35a93a7d-8bc4-5627-9377-9c1f52685da9" :question/math-academy-id "q-143389" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{1}{10}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{30}\n$$ for $y\\in [0,3]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}x]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\frac{1}{3}+{y}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{30},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,3].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[3{x}^{2}+9] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{10},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [0,3],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}\\ne {{(\\frac{3}{10}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{10})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{1}{10}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{30})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is *not* true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2477ece8-3c93-5cf3-a027-6e1d994ee810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594944} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9641fd-6a99-52d2-a038-a27c8a92dab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595362} {:answer/id #uuid "ab568d44-e083-51ac-8c33-3b2d43a1748b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595414} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6b2b8b-e68e-5c37-805a-0082a4f68233" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595424} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d94898-3a0a-52c4-b554-9228db1d7e0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595631}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595414} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa3a7747-9a93-5b74-ad4d-770bdac2d761" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3f3bef0c-4220-5154-b108-6ceab0c1664b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143370" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}(x+1), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{y}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(x+1)\\sqrt{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,2]\\times [0,1].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}(x+1)\\sqrt{y}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{8}(x+1)\\sqrt{y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3(x+1)}{8}{[\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{4}(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{x}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{4})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b1adcb8-fd8b-53d1-9954-4786bdf93004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595058} {:answer/id #uuid "65e73041-422d-53ee-a459-7a2b185dcfb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595151} {:answer/id #uuid "7594b756-5905-541e-89d5-f2f5892eb46e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595207} {:answer/id #uuid "a878f824-d3cc-5659-8286-97fb2ccc459f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595406} {:answer/id #uuid "fa92dbcd-2345-5e7c-9bee-7a4587ffb5ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595665}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595207} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d7ce98-635e-5120-a003-0bef6d37cb22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "511a04fd-28b5-5b53-b5fd-2cb1754e48d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-143360" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡9, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 1,0\\le Y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡9).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{e}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{32}{e}^{y}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,2]\\times [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡9].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{32}{e}^{y}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡9 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1,0\\le Y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡9) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}\\frac{3}{32}{e}^{y}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{32}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}}[{e}^{y}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c206f5-521d-5fc5-846f-aaeb7ac83a66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{3}(y+2), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594877} {:answer/id #uuid "138de91e-b2cd-5891-8e3b-fbb8cee68248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{3}(y+2), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594887} {:answer/id #uuid "1abe4581-163f-52ab-b253-03b015325c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}({x}^{3}-y), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594915} {:answer/id #uuid "8089c8fb-c451-57da-ae1e-06193c395f2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using only the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595249} {:answer/id #uuid "9379fded-8ac5-558f-995e-3d043d371bda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{2}y, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595325}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595249} :answer-field/id #uuid "f72c53a5-9604-53d2-8cb8-1d9f054402a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "51ee8559-018f-5381-b006-a432639d178e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143346" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}(y+2), & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7ae38f-ab98-512e-85de-9299d5097713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594872} {:answer/id #uuid "1cbee373-9793-5c99-845e-2eb28a4863ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{2,4,5\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594923} {:answer/id #uuid "2ffcfd93-fdee-53de-92a7-a2e1554ac088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{2,3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594977} {:answer/id #uuid "d09e437c-84d7-5f3d-afda-e60f0b090f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{1,4\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595522} {:answer/id #uuid "e46fb443-b3de-5432-b05f-00ccbd024ef9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{1,4,5\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595584}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594977} :answer-field/id #uuid "a71b8623-3c86-559b-9916-3956bb45d788" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7c882e01-5763-5986-83e7-9b0a2a5ec12d" :question/math-academy-id "q-174357" :question/problem "![](images/90/908a7c34818915cc0cf1709f82d0b705b843b57ac4e437eb5208748c6f9161a9.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the subset $A$ of $U=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$ whose indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ is shown above?" :question/worked-solution "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $2$ and $3$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{2,3\\}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08911307-498e-557d-b69f-4c4a6f366299" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594845} {:answer/id #uuid "7434c3a2-d090-54c9-93bb-ac56038eccc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595204} {:answer/id #uuid "8bcbbcb1-4f96-5529-81f1-009860e973c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595291} {:answer/id #uuid "981c7d7f-752a-5f9e-8826-61c574abd52f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595349} {:answer/id #uuid "bd68ecf2-51d1-5121-88f9-adc4454785ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595463}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595204} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b1a33b-ccee-5108-91bf-b5da0f45bda8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8043a273-a319-5370-970a-271d28c684d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-143585" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{3}{2},$ find $\\text{E}[2Y-4X].$" :question/worked-solution "We're given $\\text{E}[Y].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $X$ when $0\\le x\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}y]_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $X$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2Y-4X] & =2\\,\\text{E}[Y]+(-4)\\,\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =2\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-4\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02255f96-c42e-578e-870b-69261aa70626" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594826} {:answer/id #uuid "34fafad2-ce97-573c-80a7-f2d04ffcdef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594989} {:answer/id #uuid "402ea738-302d-5168-943e-cad9a0496ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595024} {:answer/id #uuid "96c72117-5d39-5c9e-8f0c-8df2fa7bba89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595343} {:answer/id #uuid "f8730e27-4bfa-52b8-8dfc-6113e4acf205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595653}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594826} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5e7d411-235f-5db3-8199-89400f7730ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "90221e5f-e86e-53c2-ae31-0642a68abcc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-143388" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}x+1$ for $y\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[xy+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =x+\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [0,1],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{x+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}\\ne {{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is *not* true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements is true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595966 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ef6c2b1-d83a-5628-8a2a-6f40049fd638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595072} {:answer/id #uuid "586f5e4e-178e-57a7-88ab-9195f9a564e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595107} {:answer/id #uuid "66567a3d-a46a-50b7-8123-9beaa0735424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595152} {:answer/id #uuid "763e1ab5-11d5-542c-ad11-60a88e54507c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595208} {:answer/id #uuid "f42ed87f-f2ca-5507-a320-1a7d8156321f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595638}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595152} :answer-field/id #uuid "a80b143e-2961-5879-ab42-ee41f4963ee7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "93ebdec3-2f38-57e1-aa0d-9e37c1dd24af" :question/math-academy-id "q-149615" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x{y}^{2}+2}{6}, & 2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expected value of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal density function for $Y$ when $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{x{y}^{2}+2}{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2x]}_{x=2}^{x=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(8{y}^{2}+8-2{y}^{2}-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(6{y}^{2}+4) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot ({y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+\\frac{2}{3}y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
